{"name": "add_theory", "arguments": {"claim": "Employers and wellness programs will adopt a non\u2011contact sleep\u2011sensing device for their workforce if they perceive it as a cost\u2011effective way to improve productivity and reduce health\u2011related absenteeism, because sleep quality is linked to workplace performance.", "who_benefits": "Employers seeking to enhance employee well\u2011being and reduce costs associated with poor sleep.", "why_behaviour_changes": "Growing recognition that sleep deprivation affects productivity and health, combined with the availability of wearable sensors that can be integrated into wellness programs, creates a market for sleep\u2011focused devices that can be deployed at",
"how_value_is_captured": "ResMed can capture value by selling the device to employers through HR platforms or wellness providers at a volume price, offering subscription services for data analytics and sleep coaching, and by bundling it with existing sleep\u2011clinic",
"assumptions": ["Employers are willing to invest in sleep health as a cost\u2011saving measure.", "Sleep data can be used to identify productivity losses and target interventions.", "ResMed can provide a device that meets privacy and compliance standards for workplace use."], "predictions": ["If offered to employers at a price of \u20ac120 per device, at least 15% of target employers will purchase within 12 weeks.", "Purchase intent will be higher among employers with existing wellness programs.", "Willingness to pay will be influenced by the number of employees and the perceived impact on productivity."]}}

